  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 417 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over far southeast Saskatchewan,\nwith trough dropping into North Dakota. Upper level analysis\nplaces low stacked over southeast Saskatchewan, with \nsouthwesterly flow over our area. A few very weak showers are \nnoted over parts of central and northeast North Dakota with this\nsystem, while southwesterly to westerly winds continue to be on\nthe increase behind the trough.\n\nFor today, westerly winds will continue to be on the increase\nthis morning as aforementioned low moves to the east, passing \nover Lake Winnipeg around mid-day, and over the Manitoba/Ontario\nborder by evening. Tightest pressure gradient and best mixing\ndown of stronger winds will be generally to the north of\nInterstate 94 where gusts to 55 are expected, though even along\nand to the south it will be windy with gusts to 45 mph. Along\nwith the general concerns that come with wind, minimum \nafternoon humidity values will mainly be in the 30s over \nsouthwest and south central North Dakota, with possibly some \nupper 20s over portions of Bowman and Adams Counties. Therefore,\nnear critical fire weather conditions are expected over much of\nsouthwest and south central North Dakota. To the north, higher \nrelative humidity values are expected. \n\nWinds will then diminish this evening. In the meantime a\nnotable short wave will will swing through in the flow behind \nthe low pushing into our area this evening. This will likely\nbring showers to primarily northern portions our our area in the\nevening and overnight moving easterly, and possibly a few \nmodest thunderstorms early on.\n\nQuieter and cooler weather is expected on Wednesday, but modest\nridging over the area Thursday will bring notably warmer \ntemperatures, with highs in the 80s over most locations, with a\nfew spots possibly touching 90. In addition, notably drier air\nmoves in resulting in quite low humidity values, especially over\nthe southwest. Therefore, near critical to critical fire weather\nconditions will be possible given an expected westerly to\nsouthwesterly breeze.\n\nA cold front moves through overnight into early Friday bringing\na breezy to windy day on Friday with increasing northwesterly \nwinds. This also will usher in notably cooler air, with highs \nexpected to possibly be 10 to 15 degrees below average through \nthe weekend into the start of the following work week.",
